# Bourree in E minor, from the Suite in E minor, BWV 996 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: the most famous movement of the suite, a two-part bourree:
# a quick, cheerful duple dance starting on a quarter upbeat, the tune
# in eighths and quarters over a bass that walks and answers it. Light
# and springy, two beats to the bar, with a lute's clarity.
#
# The suite: usually called the Lute Suite No. 1 (or in E minor), but no
# autograph survives. An early copy (J. G. Walther's, about 1710-17,
# is the one usually cited, though one source places the copy in the
# Krebs collection) is headed "Praeludio con la Suite da Gio: Bast.
# Bach", and an unidentified hand added "aufs Lauten Werck": for the
# lute-harpsichord (Lautenwerk).
# Most scholars think that was the intended instrument (E minor sits
# awkwardly on the Baroque lute's D minor tuning), though it is played
# today mostly on guitar and lute. This Mutopia edition (after the 1921
# Denkmaeler alter Lautenkunst) names both: "Lute or Lute-Harpsichord".
#
# Time signature and bars: 2/2, a quarter upbeat (pickup), 24 bars:
# first half bars 1-8 (from the upbeat, ending on beat 3 of bar 8),
# second half from the upbeat in bar 8 to bar 24 (ending on beat 3).
# The score has both repeat signs; the MIDI plays each half once, so
# the repeats are written out below with play.
#
# Tempo: half note = 72, with both repeats 96 half notes, about 1:20.
# Track times with repeats (MusicBrainz recording search): Narciso
# Yepes 1:33 (about 62), Andres Segovia about 1:30 in several releases
# (64), Hopkinson Smith, lute, 1:31 (63), Konrad Junghaenel, lute, 1:22
# (70), Jason Vieaux 1:23 (69), Sharon Isbin 1:20 (72), Kazuhito
# Yamashita 1:10 (82). Gustav Leonhardt (0:56) and Paul Galbraith
# (0:48) are much shorter, perhaps without all the repeats; not
# confirmed. I found no listing for Bream or Williams. 72 is at the
# lively end of the lutenists and guitarists, which suits a plucked
# chip sound with no sustain.
#
# Structure (from the Mutopia score, checked against the MIDI; keys are
# my reading):
#   0.75-4   E minor: the tune, its first phrase closing on E (4).
#   5-8      the tune again, turning to G major; cadence in G at 8.
#   8.75-12  second half: from G toward A minor (cadence at 12).
#   13-16    toward B minor, with a trilled cadence (15-16).
#   17-20    a falling sequence, one bar each, back toward E minor; the
#            dominant, B, at 20.
#   21-24    the close in E minor.
#
# The performance (play, below):
#   0.75-8.75    first half              16.75-32.75  second half
#   8.75-16.75   first half, repeated    32.75-48.75  second half,
#                                                     repeated
# so the score's bar N is performed bar N and N+8 in the first half,
# and N+8 and N+24 in the second.
#
# Range: the bass goes down to E2 and D2, below the chip's B2; those
# notes are lifted an octave, so some bass leaps come out narrower or
# turned round. The dynamics follow the phrases regardless.

pickup 4
tempo 72 2           # half note, two beats to the bar
play 0.75-8.75 0.75-8.75 8.75-24.75 8.75-24.75

voice mid 0          # two parts: tune and bass

dyn 0.75 f           # first half, first time: the dance, full
dyn 8.75 mp          # first half repeated: an echo
dyn 16.75 f          # second half, first time
dyn 32.75 mp         # second half repeated: an echo
dyn 44.75 mf         # score 21-24: the last close, firmer than the echo

rit 1.5 0.75         # into the final E
